--rust-head
struct Rt;
--rust
fn main() {
    // THE TABLE ITSELF, folded to one number. A `defdata` emits data and no
    // functions -- `flat-array`'s accessors are inline templates -- so what a
    // probe can check here is that three targets emitted the SAME data, which
    // for 1528 integers is exactly the thing a reader cannot check by eye.
    let mut h: i64 = 0;
    for v in CASE_RANGES.iter() { h = h.wrapping_mul(31).wrapping_add(*v as i64); }
    let mut g: i64 = 0;
    for v in CASE_FULL.iter() { g = g.wrapping_mul(31).wrapping_add(*v as i64); }
    println!("{} {} {} {} {} {}",
             CASE_RANGES.len(), h, CASE_FULL.len(), g, CASE_UPPER_LEN, CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN);
}
--java-head
    static final class Rt {}
--java
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long h = 0;
        for (int v : CASE_RANGES) h = h * 31 + v;
        long g = 0;
        for (int v : CASE_FULL) g = g * 31 + v;
        System.out.println(CASE_RANGES.length + " " + h + " " + CASE_FULL.length + " " + g
                           + " " + CASE_UPPER_LEN + " " + CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN);
    }
--csharp-head
    internal sealed class Rt {}
--csharp
    static void Main() {
        long h = 0;
        foreach (int v in CaseRanges) h = unchecked(h * 31 + v);
        long g = 0;
        foreach (int v in CaseFull) g = unchecked(g * 31 + v);
        System.Console.WriteLine(CaseRanges.Length + " " + h + " " + CaseFull.Length + " " + g
                                 + " " + CaseUpperLen + " " + CaseFullUpperLen);
    }
--expect
1528 -3643023067884571207 515 5989950694116713709 200 102
--expect-why
COMPUTED FROM `casetable.kin` BY A SECOND READER, not read off a run. A
python script parsed the two `:data` literals straight out of the source and
folded them with the same `h = h*31 + v` over 64-bit wrapping:

  len(case-ranges) = 1528 integers  (382 runs of stride 4)
  fold             = -3643023067884571207
  len(case-full)   =  515 integers  (103 runs of stride 5)
  fold             =  5989950694116713709

That is the point of the check. The table is DERIVED from the host JDK's
Unicode data by `bin/casetable --derive` and is the artifact of record; what
can go wrong afterwards is the EMITTER -- kin dropping, reordering or
sign-flipping an entry on its way into three languages. Folding the source
independently catches that, where three generated copies of a mangled table
would agree.

THE LAST TWO ARE NOT RESTATEMENTS OF THE `defconst`s, which would check a
constant against itself. `CASE_UPPER_LEN` and `CASE_FULL_UPPER_LEN` are the
boundary a BINARY SEARCH splits on, so they are checked against the shape of
the data instead:

  case-ranges runs 0..199 ascend by start and end at [125218 125251 -34 1];
  run 200 starts over at [65 90 32 1] and the rest ascend again. 200 is
  exactly where the uppercase section ends.

  case-full runs 0..101 ascend and end at [64279 ...]; run 102 starts over at
  [304 2 105 775 0] -- the Turkish dotted I, the only lowercase full mapping.
  102 is exactly where that section ends.

A boundary off by one leaves both halves unsorted at the seam, which a binary
search does not report: it returns a miss, and `upper-case` silently declines
to map a character. That is the failure this field is for. Verified by script;
both halves were also checked to be strictly ascending on `start`, which is
what the search requires and what nothing else asserts.
